# TTOS CTF CAN dashboard configuration (read by the systemd unit).

# Listen address. On the car the console is served on the WiFi AP address, port 80
# (reachable only over the AP). This must match the AP address in 30-wlan-ap.network
# / the per-car provisioned value. The service retries the bind until wlan0 is up.
TTOS_DASH_ADDR=192.168.244.1:80

# Comma-separated CAN interfaces to monitor.
TTOS_DASH_IFACES=candrive,candiag

# Comma-separated CAN interfaces whose RAW FRAMES may be streamed to the UI.
# EMPTY = none, and that is correct for a competition car: a locked panel showing
# live DRIVE-bus traffic hands contestants the C2 corpus by passive sniffing, which
# defeats the gateway policy. The bus tabs are not drawn at all when this is empty.
# Set to can0,can1 on a bench image when you want the frame view back.
# Phase 5 replaces this all-or-nothing gate with per-session unlock tiers.
# Phase 5: per-session tier gating is now the real control (DIAG after C1, DRIVE
# after C2), so the buses are listed here and the tier decides who sees them. A
# LOCKED panel still gets no frame tabs at all -- /api/info filters the list by
# tier, and the SSE stream re-checks per event.
#
# Emptying this remains an absolute KILL SWITCH: nothing streams regardless of
# tier. Keep it as the independent second gate, not as the primary one.
TTOS_DASH_FRAMES=candrive,candiag

# TTOS_DASH_DRIVE IS RETIRED (2026-08-04) AND IS NO LONGER READ.
#
# It named a second, separate socket used only by the control pad, and setting it
# empty read as "this car is read-only". It never was. The heartbeat, node
# provisioning, the UDS pivot routine, the C2 bridge and the C3 telematics relay
# all write to the drive bus through the CTF layer's own socket and were never
# gated by it -- so the bus was always writable, and emptying this only disabled
# the operator's own buttons. Provisioning emptied it on every competition car,
# which meant the e-stop could not zero the step buffers or drop the 12V rail
# while the panel still reported success.
#
# Drive authority is the tier-3 session gate (redeem the Challenge 3 code), which
# is what ARCHITECTURE.md section 8 has always described. Same change already made
# for TTOS_DASH_FRAMES when per-session tiers replaced its all-or-nothing switch.
#
# The drive interface itself is TTOS_CTF_DRIVE_IF (default candrive).

# Motion tuning. The motor nodes run a consumed step buffer: each command ADDS
# STEP_CHUNK steps to a buffer (capped at the firmware STEPS_MAX, ~2.5x a chunk =
# 250) that the node drains as it steps, so dropped/jittered CAN frames don't stall
# it. rpm sets speed (lower = gentler). Each KEEPALIVE_MS adds ~2x what's consumed
# between frames, so the buffer fills to the cap and rides a couple of missed
# frames; release/stop clears it. Bigger STEP_CHUNK/STEPS_MAX = smoother through
# loss but longer coast if a release is missed.
#
# DO NOT LOWER THESE TO ~40-70 RPM "to be gentler". At 200 full steps/rev and no
# microstepping, rpm maps to step rate as rpm/60*200:
#     100 rpm -> 333 steps/s      50 rpm -> 167 steps/s
# 167 steps/s sits in the steppers' MID-BAND RESONANCE region (~100-200 pps in full
# step). The rotor oscillates about each detent instead of settling: it grinds
# audibly, torque collapses, and it drops steps -- so it runs slower than commanded
# on top of the intended reduction. Turns ran at 50 until 2026-08-02 and sounded
# like they were tearing themselves apart; 100 is clean. Tune UPWARD from here.
#
# The textbook fix is microstepping on the driver, but that would break Challenge 1:
# the 102-step 45-degree pivot is derived from 200 full steps/rev, and at 1/8
# microstepping the same arc needs 816 steps -- past the firmware's STEPS_MAX of 250,
# which violates the "pivot is always one command per wheel" rule. Re-derive
# TTOS_PIVOT_STEPS and STEPS_MAX first if you ever enable it.
TTOS_DASH_RPM=100
TTOS_DASH_TURN_RPM=100
TTOS_DASH_KEEPALIVE_MS=150
TTOS_DASH_STEP_CHUNK=100

# ---- C3 telematics relay ---------------------------------------------------
# Authenticated raw-CAN relay on the DRIVE bus. Empty = disabled.
#
# BIND THE AP ADDRESS, NEVER 0.0.0.0. The whole premise of C3 is that the relay is
# reachable over the car's own WiFi and nowhere else; 0.0.0.0 would put an
# internet-protocol path to the drive bus on the wired network too, which is the
# exact hole socketcand was removed for. This must match TTOS_DASH_ADDR's host.
#
# 29537 is deliberately adjacent to socketcand's 29536 -- plausible for a
# telematics service, and discoverable by a port scan or via DID 0xF1A1.
# The key is SHA-256(vin:serial:fleet_salt)[:16]; see relay.go and the
# deriveServiceKey() shipped in the panel JS.
TTOS_RELAY_ADDR=192.168.244.1:29537

# ---- C1 pivot routine ------------------------------------------------------
# The UDS pivot routine (RoutineControl 0x0201) rotates the car in place and
# returns the C1 unlock code. 102 steps = 45 deg at 0.4411 deg/step; the arc does
# not depend on rpm.
#
# TTOS_PIVOT_RPM: 75, chosen 2026-08-03. Provisional -- tune on a real car.
#
# It has to satisfy TWO constraints that pull in opposite directions:
#
#   MECHANICAL. rpm x 3.33 = steps/sec at 200 full steps/rev. 50 rpm = 167 pps,
#   inside the steppers' mid-band resonance (~100-200 pps): it grinds AND drops
#   steps, so a 102-step pivot does not actually turn 45 degrees. 100 rpm = 333 pps
#   is verified clean on hardware. 75 rpm = 250 pps should clear the band -- this is
#   the part that still needs a real drivetrain to confirm.
#
#   CHALLENGE. The C2 solve replays CAPTURED pivot frames, which structurally carry
#   this rpm. C3 fires on sustained same-dir commanding at an rpm that is NOT this
#   one. So this value must be one a contestant would never pick to drive at --
#   otherwise they forge at the same speed, match the pivot rpm, and evade C3
#   without ever knowing it. That is why 100 is not an option despite being quiet:
#   100 is exactly what someone would choose to teleoperate at.
#
# 75 is above the resonance band and an odd enough value that nobody drives at it.
# If a real car still grinds at 75, try 150 (500 pps) before going back to 50.
#
# MUST MATCH PIVOT_RPM in firmware/TinytrekBMS -- it is the C3 discriminator there.
# Changing it after the fleet is flashed means reflashing eight BMS nodes.
TTOS_PIVOT_STEPS=102
TTOS_PIVOT_RPM=75

# ---- CTF service layer -----------------------------------------------------
# Bus ROLE NAMES, not numbers. The drive bus is the HIGHER-numbered interface on
# this hardware -- verified 2026-08-01, and the opposite of the original design doc.
#   DRIVE = candrive   motors, BMS, heartbeat. Contestants only reach it in C3.
#   DIAG  = candiag   the side tap. Contestants live here. Silent until one is plugged
#                  in; transmits fail ENOBUFS until then (a lone node gets no ACK).
TTOS_CTF_ENABLE=1
TTOS_CTF_DRIVE_IF=candrive
TTOS_CTF_DIAG_IF=candiag

# Per-car challenge identity (VIN, ECU serial, fleet salt, CRC Data IDs, unlock
# codes), staged here by first-boot provisioning. Mode 600, root-owned. Missing
# values are reported LOUDLY at startup -- a car that boots without them answers
# DID reads with nothing and silently breaks C3.
TTOS_CTF_IDENTITY=/etc/ttos/provision.src

# Pi liveness heartbeat (CAN 0x100) for the motor nodes' status LEDs. They show
# READY (green) only while this keeps arriving and the BMS reports 12V on; losing
# it gives a double-blink red, so you can tell "Pi not talking" from "no 12V" by
# looking at the robot. Must stay well under the firmware's 1s timeout. 0 = off.
#
# Transmitted UNCONDITIONALLY by the CTF layer. Byte 1 carries the detector ARM
# mask (bit0 C2, bit1 C3) that the BMS mirrors; see CHALLENGE-PLAN.md section D.
#
# IT IS NOT AN INTERLOCK. This line used to say the motor nodes "refuse to move"
# without it. They do not: the sketches drain their step buffer on `stepsLeft > 0`
# alone, and heartbeatOK() only drives the status LED. Stopping the car is
# electrical -- the BMS dropping the 12V rail. Do not design anything around
# heartbeat loss halting the wheels.
TTOS_DASH_HEARTBEAT_MS=200

# Battery gauge: the BMS transmits Vbat (mV, uint16 BE, LOADED terminal voltage) on
# this CAN ID; the gauge maps battmin..battmax mV (RESTING cell voltages) to 0..100%.
# 3S pack: 2.8 V/cell (8.4 V) 0% .. 4.1 V/cell (12.3 V) 100%.
# LOAD_OFFSET_MV is added to the measured reading to undo the ~constant droop under
# the Pi's steady load (so a full pack reads ~100%). Calibrate against a DMM:
# offset ~= (resting battery volts) - (volts shown on the bus while running).
TTOS_DASH_BATT_ID=0x116
TTOS_DASH_BATT_MIN_MV=8400
TTOS_DASH_BATT_MAX_MV=12300
TTOS_DASH_BATT_LOAD_OFFSET_MV=1000
